\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Effect of qubit relaxation during readout on the assignment of the pre- and post-measurement state. A qubit initially in $\mathinner {|{1}\rangle }$ decays to $\mathinner {|{0}\rangle }$ early (left) or late (right) during the readout; the top row shows the qubit state $\mathinner {|{\psi }\rangle }$ during the readout. (a),(b)~Conventional assignment: the demodulated signal (grey; dotted: noiseless response) is averaged over the integration window (shaded) and thresholded (dashed line), which gives a single outcome $m_n$ that is used as both the pre- and the post-measurement state. The pre-measurement state is wrong in~(a) and the post-measurement state is wrong in~(b). (c),(d)~Trace-based inference (blue line) detects the decay (dashed line) and returns separate outcomes $m_n^{\mathrm  {pre}}$ and $m_n^{\mathrm  {post}}$, which are correct in both cases. The traces are synthetic and for illustration only. }}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{2}{Effect of qubit relaxation during readout on the assignment of the pre- and post-measurement state. A qubit initially in $\ket {1}$ decays to $\ket {0}$ early (left) or late (right) during the readout; the top row shows the qubit state $\ket {\psi }$ during the readout. (a),(b)~Conventional assignment: the demodulated signal (grey; dotted: noiseless response) is averaged over the integration window (shaded) and thresholded (dashed line), which gives a single outcome $m_n$ that is used as both the pre- and the post-measurement state. The pre-measurement state is wrong in~(a) and the post-measurement state is wrong in~(b). (c),(d)~Trace-based inference (blue line) detects the decay (dashed line) and returns separate outcomes $m_n^{\mathrm {pre}}$ and $m_n^{\mathrm {post}}$, which are correct in both cases. The traces are synthetic and for illustration only}{figure.1}{}}
