\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Ramsey measurement without qubit reset. (a)~In the no-reset protocol, the post-measurement state of readout $n-1$ is the initial state of shot $n$. Conventional assignment (above the meters) gives one outcome $m_n$ used for both states; trace-based inference (below) gives separate pre- and post-measurement states, $m_n^{\mathrm  {pre}}$ and $m_n^{\mathrm  {post}}$. (b)~Active reset applies a conditional $\pi $ pulse after a feedback latency. (c)~Illustrative Ramsey fringes (schematic, not measured data): relaxation during readout reduces the amplitude and shifts the conventional no-reset curve downward; trace-based inference recovers the ideal fringe without reset.}}{2}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:noreset_ramsey}{{2}{2}{Ramsey measurement without qubit reset. (a)~In the no-reset protocol, the post-measurement state of readout $n-1$ is the initial state of shot $n$. Conventional assignment (above the meters) gives one outcome $m_n$ used for both states; trace-based inference (below) gives separate pre- and post-measurement states, $m_n^{\mathrm {pre}}$ and $m_n^{\mathrm {post}}$. (b)~Active reset applies a conditional $\pi $ pulse after a feedback latency. (c)~Illustrative Ramsey fringes (schematic, not measured data): relaxation during readout reduces the amplitude and shifts the conventional no-reset curve downward; trace-based inference recovers the ideal fringe without reset}{figure.2}{}}
